1 NAME Gunnar /Seidenfaden/ til Borsholmgaard
2 GIVN Gunnar
2 NSFX til Borsholmgaard
2 _MARNM Seidenfaden
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 FEB 1908
2 ADDR
3 ADR1 Søndergade
3 ADR3 Varde
3 CITY Varde Sogn
3 STAE Ribe Amt
3 CTRY Denmark
1 BURI
2 DATE FEB 2001
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 FEB 2001
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Denmark
1 OCCU Cand.polit, diplomat
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.helsingorleksikon.dk/index.php/Borsholmgaard
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilde: Helsingør Leksikon: http://www.helsingorleksikon.dk/index.php/Gunnar_Seidenfaden: Gunnar Seidenfaden (1908-2001) havde ingen speciel tilknytning til området før han i 1959 købte Borsholmgaard sammen med sin hustru Alix f. A
2 CONC rnstedt. Gunnar Seidenfaden blev oprindelig uddannet cand.polit. og blev efterfølgende ansat i Udenrigsministeriet. Han fik der en karriere indenfor diplomatiet og blev i 1958 første gang ambassadør i Bangkok. Efter flere poster b
2 CONC lev han i 1971 Danmarks repræsentant ved internationale miljøkonferencer. Og det var ingen tilfældighed - han var dybt engageret i naturvidenskabelige emner - især botanik. Især fik den arktiske og den thailandske flora Seidenfade
2 CONC ns opmærksomhed. På smuk vis lykkedes det Seidenfaden at forene de to interesser, i det han senere blev en af hovedmændene bag forskellige konventioner vedr. bevarelse af dyre- og plantelivet i Europa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Efter at han i 1973 var blevet løst fra sine diplomatiske arbejdsopgaver, fik han mere tid til at dyrke sin store interesse for orkidéer. Under sine mange ekspeditioner til Thailand indsamlede han således henved 10.000 orkidéer ti
2 CONC l sit laboratorium som han havde indrettet på Borsholmgaard. Seidenfaden arbejdede i disse år på et 16 binds værk om orkidéer - bl.a. ved at dyrke videre på de indsamlede planter, der først kunne beskrives endeligt når de havde bl
2 CONC omstret. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For sin samlede indsats opnåede han den ære at få et af Danmarks miljøskibe opkaldt efter sig.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In Memoriam: Gunnar Seidenfaden (1908-2001) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Gunnar Seidenfaden, Danish diplomat, explorer, naturalist, and expert in tropical Asiatic orchids died on 9 February 2001. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunnar Seidenfaden began his long and extraordinary career as a student of botany in Greenland in 1928. His talent for organization soon made him practical manager of Dr. Lauge Koch's geological expeditions to Eastern Greenland. H
2 CONC e spent six summers doing field work, which resulted in numerous publications on arctic botany, including "The phytoplankton of the waters west of Greenland" with Julius Grøntved. In 1938, he won a Scandinavian contest for the bes
2 CONC t popular science book with Modern Arctic Exploration. However, by then he had changed his field of study to economy and political science. After graduation, he was employed by the Danish Ministry of Foreign affairs and travelled 
2 CONC extensively in the Americas.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1955, he was appointed Danish ambassador in Thailand and six other Asiatic countries and four years later ambassador in the USSR. He used his spare time for a comeback in natural history and began a collaboration with the Royal
2 CONC  Thai Forest Department, with which he arranged a number of collecting expeditions until the mid-1980s. His first large work on Thai orchids, the four-volume The Orchids of Thailand - A Preliminary List with Dr. Tem Smitinand, was
2 CONC  in fact completed in Moscow. From 1961 to 1967 Seidenfaden was head of the Juridical Department of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. After 1967 he left regular duty as a civil servant to spend more time on the orchid flora of Thai
2 CONC land, but because of his interest in natural history he had many special assignments as Danish envoy at international conferences on environmental matters, including the Washington Convention on International Trade in Endangered S
2 CONC pecies (1973), The Helsinki Convention on the Protection of Marine Environment (1979), and the Bern Convention on Protection of European Wildlife (1979). He was later much disappointed too see how the Washington Convention develop
2 CONC ed in many countries into a bureaucratic tool for harassing people trying to exchange orchid material legally - even researchers and scientific institutions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most important of Seidenfaden's numerous works on Asiatic orchids may be the monograph series Orchid Genera in Thailand. The first volume (I-III) was published in 1975, and the last one (XIV) was the impressive "59 Vandoid Gen
2 CONC era" in 1988. In this work, Seidenfaden suggested a new artificial classification of the "vandoids" based on pollinia. This is still the most usable framework for identification in this large and difficult group of orchids. A numb
2 CONC er of taxonomic and floristic notes on Thai orchids were published in the series Contributions to the Orchid Flora of Thailand" I-XIII (1969-1997). Some of the later works by Seidenfaden treated the orchid floras of surrounding co
2 CONC untries, e.g. The Orchids of Indochina and The Orchids of Peninsular Malaysia and Singapore with Jeffrey Wood, both published in 1992. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Seidenfaden's works are strictly taxonomic and floristic and have a distinct appearance because of the abundant detailed drawings, showing flowers and minute parts exactly as they appeared under the dissecting microscope. They wer
2 CONC e all drawn by himself with pencil and inked by various artists, the latest by Katja Anker, who took over the actual drawing work for the unfinished "Contribution 14". Seidenfaden disliked long, descriptive texts and often express
2 CONC ed the view that a good key, terse diagnoses, and accurate drawings are much better than elaborate descriptions. He described at least 120 new taxa and appears as author in more than 240 specific names, but he was in fact more pro
2 CONC ud of the hundreds of other names he reduced to synonyms after careful study. Generic eponyms include names as Seidenfadenia, Seidenfadeniella, Seidenfia, Gunnarella, Gunnarorchis, and Fadenia, a genus of fossil sharks from the Pe
2 CONC rmian period described in 1932. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gunnar Seidenfaden's living collection is kept in the Botanic Garden of Copenhagen, where it was recently transferred to a new greenhouse. However, he worked almost entirely at his home in Borsholm, a little town 50 km north of Co
2 CONC penhagen. His collection of orchids preserved in liquid with about 10,000 specimens was stored in the basement in strict taxonomic order. This meant that to see Dr. Seidenfaden and his material one had to visit "Borsholmgaard", wh
2 CONC ich became an international orchid science salon. Many orchid people from all over the world -- amateurs, students, and academic professionals -- share pleasant memories about the hospitality of Gunnar and his wife Lulu, the speci
2 CONC al atmosphere of the place, and his never-failing enthusiasm for science, politics, natural history, and orchids.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finn N. Rasmussen
