1 NAME Lotte /Laserstein/
2 GIVN Lotte
2 SURN Laserstein
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 28 NOV 1898
2 ADDR
3 CITY Preussich Holland
3 STAE East Prussia
3 CTRY German Empire
1 DEAT
2 DATE 21 JAN 1993
2 ADDR
3 CITY Kalmar
3 STAE Kalmar County
3 CTRY Sweden
1 OCCU Painter
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lotte_Laserstein
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.daxermarschall.com/en/portfolio-view/lotte-laserstein/
2 CONT
2 CONT https://londonartweek.co.uk/lottelasersteinweimar/?mc_cid=ca7f478350&mc_eid=f7e5381e0a
2 CONT
2 CONT Painter Lotte Laserstein, who moved to Sweden when her career in Germany was thwarted by Nazism, gained prominence in the European art world for her realist portraiture style.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her initial art training in a school run by one of her aunts in Berlin, Laserstein became one of the few female students at the prestigious Berlin Academy of Fine Arts. She studied there from 1919 through 1925.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laserstein became an accomplished realist painter, winning the Academy’s gold medal for her work. She soon had her own pupils, and her first solo exhibition at a Berlin gallery in 1930 garnered critical praise. To supplement her i
2 CONC ncome, Laserstein took various jobs making decorative art and, most significantly, illustrating an anatomy text.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite her increasing success, the rise of Nazism in Germany began to affect Laserstein’s life. Because her family had been Jewish, Laserstein’s mother’s apartment and many of her valuables were confiscated by the state. It becam
2 CONC e difficult for Laserstein to find artists’ materials, and in 1935 she was forced to close her studio.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1937, Laserstein moved to Stockholm, where she remained for the rest of her life. She became a member of the Swedish Academy of Arts and developed a reputation as a popular and respected portraitist.  NMWA Exhibitions
2 CONT
