2 CONT Mosaic Religious Community Table of Contents [ skjul] 1 History 2 Search options 3 Printed literature 4 In the archives 5 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT '''History'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jews had originally not allowed to come to Denmark. Before 1600, there are Jews in Altona , and they get their privilege confirmed in 1641 by Christian IV, like Christian IV permit Jewish settlement in Rendsburg and Friedrichstadt
2 CONC  as part of its industrial policy. In the course of 18 century also arise Jewish congregations in the kingdom : Copenhagen, Fredericia and other towns. Genealogy in Jewish families will always be a challenge:
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondly, the Jewish communities first from 1814 to keep records of births, marriages and deaths following the same recipe as in the national church parish registers. However, there are burial records of Copenhagen from 1771; Juli
2 CONC us Margolinsky: "Jewish deaths in Denmark 1693-1976" summarizes in a book, what records and tombstone says. There are birth records before 1814, but only with information about child secondly, research will inevitably lead one bey
2 CONC ond its borders.
2 CONT
2 CONT To understand the sources, you should know that there are religious differences between the askenaniske German Jews and the Sephardic Portuguese Jews. these differences affect how the churches organize themselves and for who marri
2 CONC es whom Search options
2 CONT
2 CONT Jewish Genealogical Society of Denmark gathers experience with genealogy of Jewish families. JGS cooperate with Hamburger Gesellschaft für jüdische Genealogie eV , which also covers the [Altona] There is established a Jewish Genea
2 CONC logical Database - membership of JGS required to access the database.  Margolinsky book and thorough research on the Jewish cemeteries is why Tom Brøndsted database Jewish funerals, as far as possible, linking information on the g
2 CONC rave with a photo and mark the location on the cemetery. If you want to use Jewish Funerals, you must understand Margolinsky listing of names: '''Wallich''', Henriette, for Cantor, g Nathan Emanuel, d 1893,13.5: Henriette Cantor, 
2 CONC wife of Nathan Emanuel Wallich, died 13.5.1893
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Jewish men 1735-1810, list of paid kopulationspenge Copenhagen City Archives ; information about the spouse is available through the Jewish Genealogical Database
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Printed literature''
2 CONT
2 CONT Julius Margolinsky: Jewish deaths in Denmark 1693-1976. Copenhagen 1978 ("'''Margoteket'''")
2 CONT
2 CONT Lausten, Martin Schwarz: The Pious and the Jews. Attitudes toward Judaism and Jews in Denmark in pietismens time (1700-1760). Copenhagen 2000
2 CONT
2 CONT Lausten, Martin Schwarz: Information in Church and Synagogue. The relationship between Christians and Jews in the Danish Enlightenment (1760-1814). Copenhagen 2002 Martin Schwarz Lausten books are not primarily genealogy, but cont
2 CONC ains an approximate complete Registry of converted Jews until the 1814th Martin Schwarz Lausten has also published books on the period after the 1814th
2 CONT
2 CONT Thyge Svenstrup & Vello Helk: The Mosaic Religious Community in Copenhagen with disused Religious Community in the province. Registry. Copenhagen 1993: the records from the Mosaic Religious Community, handed over to the Provincial
2 CONC  Archives of Zealand in 1968 and the RA in 1981. Information is now also available on Daisy
2 CONT
2 CONT Solomon, J. & J. Fischer (eds.): memorial in honor of the 100-anniversary of the device, 29 March 1814, Copenhagen in 1914 with the reproduction of selected records
2 CONT
2 CONT '''At the archives'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ward Protocols (similar to "church books") Switching Protocols ; switching system was an internal matter until 1814: German Jews Portuguese Jews
2 CONT
2 CONT Applications for Royal. Passage Letters (comes closest to the residence); applications often contain references to relatives and other compounds that have already established themselves in Denmark reprints / private press by J. Fi
2 CONC scher and others: eg pedigrees  With thanks to Tom Brøndsted
2 CONT
